These stones were placed here by magick. You will not move them by human means.	這些石頭是魔法安放於此的，你無法以凡人之力搬動它們。
Easy to do, but to little purpose.	做起來容易，卻毫無用處。
You are out of the Forest Perilous with an open sky overhead.	你已走出派瑞樂斯森林，頭頂是一片開闊的天空。
I suspect your mule is more intelligent than she appears.	我猜你的騾子，其實比看起來聰明。
The larger trees have been left behind in the forest. These are mere shrubs in comparison.	高大的樹木已留在森林裡了，這些相比之下不過是灌木叢。
The magical glow around the stone must mean that you have solved its riddle.	石頭周圍的魔光，想必是你已解開了它的謎語。
That effect is the mystical barrier which prevents you from climbing the Tor.	那股力量正是阻止你攀上山丘的神秘結界。
Where? I see nothing.	在哪裡？我什麼也沒看見。
This clump of rock looks quite ordinary.	這叢岩石看來相當尋常。
There are five of these standing stones.	這裡共有五塊立石。
As you have cleverly realized, these five stones were once men. Their riddles mark them as poets.	正如你機敏地看出，這五塊石頭曾經是人。他們出的謎語，透露出詩人的身分。
I see no other human being here.	我在此處不見其他人影。
Have you forgotten already? You sent Gawaine back to Camelot upon it.	你這麼快就忘了？你讓高文騎著牠回肯萊特去了。
I would hazard that your sudden disappearance spooked it, and that it is halfway home by now.	我猜你突然消失，把牠嚇著了，此刻大概已經走了一半的路回家。
Beyond the standing stones the base of the tor rises steeply.	越過這些立石，山丘的坡基便陡然上升。
You have neither the time or tools for such a venture.	你既無時間，也無工具，做不了這等事。
This is not a barrier that you can hack your way through with Excaliber.	這道結界，不是揮舞聖劍便能劈開的。
I doubt a stone has much use for it.	我想石頭用不上這東西。
She looks content grazing her way through a goodly section of Glastonbury Tor.	牠看來心滿意足地一路吃著草，啃過了格列斯登伯利山好一片草地。
No need. She will follow now, I think.	不必了，我想牠現在會跟上來。
She does not seem to pay much attention.	牠似乎沒怎麼理會。
Mules are obstinate creatures. At best, you can hope she will follow of her own will.	騾子生性倔強，你至多只能盼牠自願跟隨。
Bend your will to your mission. Everything else can wait for the time being.	把心思放回你的使命上，其餘的事都可以暫且擱下。
There is the smell of grass and fresh air.	空氣中有青草與清新的氣息。
Had you been turned to stone, would you appreciate such a thing?	若你被化成了石頭，還會在意這種事嗎？
I see no poet, unless you perceive something that I do not.	我沒看見什麼詩人，除非你看出了我沒看出的東西。
Nothing happens. Perhaps you are not close enough to one of the stoned poets.	什麼也沒發生。或許你離某個化石詩人還不夠近。
With the spell broken, the stones maintain a sullen silence.	咒語已解，石頭只剩一片悶悶的沉默。
Move closer to a stone.	靠近某塊石頭。
Arthur, this is no time to ask me for something as frivolous as a riddle.	亞瑟，此刻不是向我討謎語這等閒事的時候。
There is no man here.	此處無人。
Arthur? Arthur! Where are you?	亞瑟？亞瑟！你在哪裡？
Once we were poets, Men free and gay, The witch has left us Stony and grey, And now we pose riddles, To block the way.	我們曾是詩人，自由自在逍遙；女巫將我們留下，化為灰石一片荒涼；如今我們出謎，只為擋住去路。
Hmmph. If that is a sample of their poetry, they deserve their fate.	哼，若這便是他們詩才的樣本，那也是自作自受。
There are five of these stones, each roughly man-sized. Perhaps they WERE men once.	這裡有五塊石頭，大小約莫與人相當。或許它們曾經真的是人。
The magical glow around this stone means that you have solved its riddle.	這塊石頭周圍的魔光，意味著你已解開了它的謎語。
Ah, there you are!  That was not my doing.  You have been brought by another power to the center of the ruins on Glastonbury Tor.	啊，你在這裡！這不是我的手筆。是另一股力量，把你帶到了格列斯登伯利山廢墟的中心。
The ruins offer little in the way of defense or concealment.	這片廢墟提供不了什麼防禦或掩蔽。
You are in the center of the ruins on Glastonbury Tor.	你正身處格列斯登伯利山廢墟的中心。
The ruins are nothing but bits of wall and fallen rock with only the Tor beneath them.	這片廢墟不過是斷牆殘石，底下便是山丘本身。
What remains would have been the arch of a doorway.	殘存的部分，想必曾是一道門拱。
This arch is the frame of a doorway.	這道拱是一扇門的框架。
You are in another part of the ruins.	你來到了廢墟的另一處。
You have opened the well.	你已打開了這口井。
The well lid is iron, with a solid iron bar and a lock holding it in place.	井蓋是鐵製的，以一根結實的鐵條與一把鎖固定住。
It has the shape and look of a well, though covered with a heavy iron lid.	看形狀與樣貌是口井，只是覆著一面沉重的鐵蓋。
The cold waters of the sacred well are dark and reveal nothing.	聖井中的冷水幽暗深沉，什麼也看不出。
It is fit into the iron bar and would therefore be impossible to break open by ordinary means.	它嵌合在鐵條之中，尋常手段不可能將它砸開。
A good idea, though difficult to do unless you can remove the lid.	這主意不錯，但除非能移開井蓋，否則難以做到。
It would help to be on the other side of the well.	若能到井的另一側，會有幫助。
It would help to be next to the well.	若能站在井邊，會有幫助。
Nay, if this is the thorn planted by Joseph of Arimathea, it would be tantamount to sacrilege.	不可，若這真是亞利馬太的約瑟親手栽下的荊棘，如此行事無異於褻瀆聖物。
It is full of thorns.	上面滿是荊棘。
I cannot imagine how you would do that.	我實在想不出你要如何辦到。
Besides roots and shadow, there is not much to see.	除了樹根與陰影，沒什麼好看的。
The sacred well is covered with a heavy lid and a solid iron bar in the shape of interlocked circles, held in place by a lock.	這口聖井覆著一面沉重的井蓋，上面有一根鑄成環環相扣圖案的結實鐵條，並以一把鎖固定。
We can only assume there is water in this well until it is opened.	在井打開之前，我們只能猜測裡頭是否有水。
It would help to be on the other side the well.	若能到井的另一側，會有幫助。
The covered well is in this section of the ruins.	這片廢墟裡，有一口覆蓋著的井。
There is the well and thorn tree, as before.	井與荊棘樹依然在此，一如先前。
There is the cowled figure of a monk near a covered well.	一口覆蓋著的井旁，站著一個披著兜帽的僧侶身影。
The arch is all that remains of a doorway.	這道拱，是一扇門僅存的殘跡。
The one small tree is covered in thorns.  The legend of Joseph of Arimathea has it that when he hid the Grail, he also stuck his staff into the ground and from that place a thorn tree grew.	這株小樹渾身是刺。傳說亞利馬太的約瑟藏起聖杯之時，也將手杖插入地中，那地方便長出了這株荊棘樹。
It is covered with a heavy iron lid.	它覆著一面沉重的鐵蓋。
The well is filled with cold, dark water.	井中滿是幽暗的冷水。
There is nothing more to be gained by looking at it now.	此刻再看下去，也看不出什麼名堂了。
It is held in place by a locked iron bar.	它由一根上了鎖的鐵條固定著。
The iron bar forms a design of interlocking circles.  Lovely, but too solid to break.	這鐵條鑄成環環相扣的圖案，精美，卻堅實得無法砸斷。
You could, if the well were open.	若井是開著的，你便能如此。
The well opening is upon the other side.	井口在另一側。
You cannot pick this lock.	你撬不開這把鎖。
That is not a very useful idea.	這主意用處不大。
Do your eyes fail you?  The well is closed.	你的眼睛不管用了嗎？這口井是關著的。
This well is both ancient and sacred.  A bit of decorum is called for.	這口井既古老又神聖，理當保持幾分敬重。
One does not swim in sacred wells, and this one has been revered for centuries.	沒有人會在聖井裡游泳，何況這口井已受人敬奉了數百年。
Beyond the pleasure of being stuck by them, I can see no purpose in that.	除了讓自己被刺一身之外，我看不出這麼做有什麼意義。
I cannot imagine how one would do that.	我實在想不出該如何辦到這件事。
Do your eyes fail you?  It IS open.	你的眼睛不管用了嗎？它是開著的。
The latch and lock are upon the other side.	門閂與鎖都在另一側。
To lift the lid, you must remove the bar.  To remove the bar, you must open the lock.  You will not open the lock by wishful thinking.	要掀開井蓋，得先移開鐵條；要移開鐵條，得先打開鎖。光憑一廂情願，是打不開這把鎖的。
Leave it open, Arthur, so that others who follow may drink from the sacred waters.	讓它開著吧，亞瑟，好讓後來的人也能飲得這聖水。
It is already locked.	它已經鎖上了。
The well is already unlocked.	這口井已經解鎖了。
You do not have the means.	你沒有辦法做到這件事。
Do your eyes fail you?  It IS closed.	你的眼睛不管用了嗎？它是關著的。
The lid closes from the other side.	井蓋要從另一側才能關上。
That would only damage Excaliber.	那麼做只會損傷聖劍。
What nonsense is this?	這是什麼胡言亂語？
The key turned easily and the lock is now open.	鑰匙輕易轉動，鎖已經打開了。
From the shiver that just ran through you, I gather the water was ice-cold.	從你剛才那陣顫抖看來，我猜這水冷得刺骨。
Did I not say the Grail was not here?  You have only a wet hand to show for your stubbornness.	我不是說過聖杯不在這裡嗎？你這般固執，換來的不過是一隻濕淋淋的手。
So THIS is the Mad Monk's treasure!  A beautiful, glittering crystal heart.  How cold it is to the touch!	原來這就是瘋僧侶的寶物！一顆美麗、閃著光芒的水晶心。摸起來竟是這般冰冷！
Alas, Arthur, this is the object of power that created the Spell of Circular Entrapment.  The Grail is not here.	唉，亞瑟，這正是造出困環咒的力量之物。聖杯不在此處。
You are in another part of the ruins.  There are ancient carvings of skulls in one wall.	你來到了廢墟的另一處。一面牆上刻著古老的骷髏雕飾。
"Memento mori" is a Latin phrase meaning:   "Remember that thou must die".	「Memento mori」是句拉丁文，意為：「勿忘你終有一死。」
An object, such as a skull, is commonly called a memento mori because it is a reminder of inevitable death.	像骷髏這樣的物件，一般稱作「memento mori」，因為它提醒著世人死亡終不可免。
The carved skulls are memento mori, reminders of the death that awaits us all.	這些雕刻的骷髏便是 memento mori，提醒著等待眾人的死亡。
The arch is the remnant of a doorway.	這道拱，是一扇門遺留下的殘跡。
This is no time to be plundering religious ruins.	此刻不是洗劫宗教廢墟的時候。
They are bushes.	那些是灌木叢。
They are rocks.	那些是岩石。
The flowers seem cheerful amid the ancient ruins.	這些花朵在古老的廢墟間，顯得生氣盎然。
The arch supports its roof no longer.	這道拱早已撐不起屋頂了。
Unwise.  Desecrating a sacred object is a good way to bring a curse down upon yourself.	不智之舉。褻瀆聖物，是招來詛咒上身的好辦法。
Not in this place or you will offend your own god by kneeling to false gods.	此地不可如此，否則你向偽神下跪，便是冒犯了你自己的神。
I hope your god is listening.	但願你的神正在聆聽。
The Old Ones have taken their silver and gone away.	古代幽靈已取走了他們的銀子，離去了。
The Old Ones have taken it.	古代幽靈已將它取走了。
You cannot take back what you have not given.	你未曾給出之物，如何取回？
The Monk had not the means to buy his freedom, so we kept him as our servant.  He was mad, but any servant is better than none.	那僧侶無力贖回自由，於是我們便將他留作僕役。他雖瘋癲，但有僕役總比沒有的好。
Of what are you speaking?  There is nothing like that here.	你在說什麼？此處並無這樣的東西。
What an utterly foolish notion.	這念頭實在愚蠢至極。
It is too heavy for that.	它太重了，辦不到。
It is solid stone.	這是實心的石頭。
There is nothing but grass and weeds.	這裡除了野草雜叢，什麼也沒有。
They are too old and weathered to be made out.	它們太過古老、風化得太厲害，已經辨認不出了。
An iron key lies upon the altar in place of your silver.	一把鐵鑰匙擺在祭壇上，取代了你的銀子。
In this part of the ruins there are the remains of some kind of altar.	廢墟的這一處，有座祭壇的殘跡。
Your silver coins lie upon the altar.	你的銀幣擺在祭壇上。
It is a key, Arthur.	這是一把鑰匙，亞瑟。
Some of your silver lies upon the altar.	你的一部分銀子擺在祭壇上。
It is an extremely ancient altar, of a most peculiar configuration.  Its three parts may hint at the worship of a trinity of some sort.	這是一座極為古老的祭壇，形制十分特異。它分作三個部分，或許暗示著某種三神一體的信仰。
Ask the Old Ones.	去問古代幽靈吧。
Copper is for the lowest spirits of the earth.  Gold is for young gods.  You cannot appease us with those metals.	銅是獻給地底最卑微的精怪，金是獻給年輕的神祇。這些金屬平息不了我們。
We three are the eldest servants of mother-moon.  We love most dearly the metal that gleams cold and pure like hardened moonlight.	我們三人是月亮之母最年長的僕從。我們最鍾愛那如凝結月光般，泛著冷冽純淨光澤的金屬。
It is the purest of metals.  Silver shines with the light of mother-moon.  You may win your freedom by giving us as much silver as the mother-moon had children.	那是最純淨的金屬。銀子閃耀著月亮之母的光輝。你若獻上與月亮之母子女數目相當的銀子，便能贏得自由。
Moonlight is the gift of mother-moon, so that total darkness will never rule upon the earth.	月光是月亮之母的贈禮，好讓黑暗永不能徹底統治這片大地。
Mother-moon had five children: earth, wind, water, fire and spirit.	月亮之母育有五個孩子：土、風、水、火與靈。
We do not want that!  There is only one metal we cherish.	我們不要那個！我們珍視的金屬只有一種。
Stay and be our servant and we will tell you wondrous secrets.	留下來做我們的僕役，我們便告訴你奇妙的秘密。
